\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Input linear matter power spectra at \(z_i=200\) used to generate the PL, BT\_soft, and BT\_deep initial conditions. The PL spectrum provides the standard power-law baseline. The BT spectra use the same target \(\sigma _8\) normalization and keep the PL/Eisenstein shape below their pivot scales, up to the small global normalization applied by MonofonIC; above the pivot, they depart from PL. BT\_soft departs from PL over a broader range of mildly nonlinear modes, whereas BT\_deep confines the modification to smaller scales. The spectra are evaluated at the initial redshift, \(z_i=200\). For the main \(25\,h^{-1}{\rm  Mpc}\), \(1024^3\) runs, \(k_{\rm  Ny}\simeq 129\,h\,{\rm  Mpc}^{-1}\), so the \(k_p=1\) and \(10\,h\,{\rm  Mpc}^{-1}\) pivots are well below the particle-grid Nyquist scale. Vertical dotted lines mark the BT pivot scales, while the gray high-\(k\) regions mark the conservative \(0.5k_{\rm  Ny}\)--\(k_{\rm  Ny}\) caution range and modes beyond \(k_{\rm  Ny}\).}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:power_spectrum_comparison}{{1}{3}{Input linear matter power spectra at \(z_i=200\) used to generate the PL, BT\_soft, and BT\_deep initial conditions. The PL spectrum provides the standard power-law baseline. The BT spectra use the same target \(\sigma _8\) normalization and keep the PL/Eisenstein shape below their pivot scales, up to the small global normalization applied by MonofonIC; above the pivot, they depart from PL. BT\_soft departs from PL over a broader range of mildly nonlinear modes, whereas BT\_deep confines the modification to smaller scales. The spectra are evaluated at the initial redshift, \(z_i=200\). For the main \(25\,h^{-1}{\rm Mpc}\), \(1024^3\) runs, \(k_{\rm Ny}\simeq 129\,h\,{\rm Mpc}^{-1}\), so the \(k_p=1\) and \(10\,h\,{\rm Mpc}^{-1}\) pivots are well below the particle-grid Nyquist scale. Vertical dotted lines mark the BT pivot scales, while the gray high-\(k\) regions mark the conservative \(0.5k_{\rm Ny}\)--\(k_{\rm Ny}\) caution range and modes beyond \(k_{\rm Ny}\)}{figure.1}{}}
